WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.141 --> 00:00:02.724
(upbeat music)

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:09.050
- Hey everybody, Buenos días,
my name is Raymond Simpson.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:10.480
I am a Museum Assistant here

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.653
in Entomology at the Peabody Museum.

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:16.120
I am a self-professed moth nerd

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:19.460
and I am very interested
in the biodiversity

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:23.003
and evolution of Arthropoda,
specifically insects.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:28.720
Today I am going to show
you some of our spectacular

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:31.650
diversity in butterflies and moths

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.300
that live in the neotropics,

00:00:33.300 --> 00:00:36.610
which is basically from
the Southern United States

00:00:36.610 --> 00:00:39.243
all the way down to
Argentina in the neo world.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.010
Most of the specimens are preserved

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:46.840
as dried pinned material
like you can see here.

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:48.820
Specimens that are collected in the wild,

00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:52.830
are pinned and dried and then
labels are adhered to them

00:00:52.830 --> 00:00:54.710
and they are put into the collection

00:00:54.710 --> 00:00:58.223
for scientists to come
and observe and study.

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:04.320
So one of my main passions
is butterflies and moths.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:11.390
As you can see the tropical
region of the neo world,

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:14.270
including Central and South America,

00:01:14.270 --> 00:01:16.563
has very high insect diversity.

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:21.160
Lots of like literally tens of thousands

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:22.800
of species of butterflies and moths,

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.280
which are scientifically
known as Lepidoptera,

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:27.590
occur in this region.

00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.940
Here's a good assemblage of butterflies

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:31.650
called 88 butterflies.

00:01:31.650 --> 00:01:35.840
They're called 88 butterflies
because the underside of them

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.510
have this cool 88 pattern
on their hind wing.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:44.510
So the top is very brightly
colored, kind of blue and pink.

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:49.220
The bottom has this
cool modeled 88 pattern

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:52.183
or just kind of cool zigzag
black markings on them.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:58.070
So there's several themes in butterfly

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:01.410
and moth diversity in the
tropics besides just diversity

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:04.060
and I'm going to show you
some of those things today.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:09.370
One of the most well
known types of butterflies

00:02:09.370 --> 00:02:11.220
in the tropics that you've
probably seen before

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:12.553
are called morphos.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:19.020
And morphos are very spectacular looking

00:02:19.020 --> 00:02:21.910
and there's numerous species of morpho,

00:02:21.910 --> 00:02:25.200
this is one particular species
it's very common in Brazil,

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.260
but it's found throughout South America.

00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:28.350
And they're very distinctive

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:31.120
and they have this really
beautiful iridescent pattern.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:32.970
As I angle the box up,

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:35.260
you could see the pattern,
the hue of the blue

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:36.980
changes in intensity.

00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:39.440
So each butterfly has wings coated

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:43.610
in very, very, very tiny
scales that coat the wing.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:46.160
And each of those scales
is like a reflector

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.850
and it reflects back the blue.

00:02:48.850 --> 00:02:50.500
So if you look at it under the microscope,

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:53.700
it's like a prism where it
reflects different colors

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:57.920
but it's very strongly
blue back to our eyes.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.510
And it really interesting about
these butterflies is like,

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:04.630
you think like, okay, how
are they very adaptive,

00:03:04.630 --> 00:03:06.400
being so brightly colored.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:10.190
But these butterflies
use this as an ability

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:12.410
to basically see each
other as their own species.

00:03:12.410 --> 00:03:15.070
So female butterflies of
this species look like this

00:03:15.070 --> 00:03:18.610
and males look like this,
that's called sexual dimorphism.

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:21.240
So a lot of these butterflies
will use color pattern

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:23.270
as a way to recognize individuals

00:03:23.270 --> 00:03:25.413
of their own particular species.

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:30.490
Also these butterflies
aren't always what they seem,

00:03:30.490 --> 00:03:35.490
so the underside of the morpho
butterfly is camouflaged.

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:40.550
So a lot of things in the tropics

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:43.900
will not only use bright
colors to warn predators

00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:45.230
that they don't taste good,

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:48.560
they also use camouflage
to protect themselves

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:49.393
when they're at rest.

00:03:49.393 --> 00:03:52.240
So these butterflies will
sit kind of like this.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:54.390
So I'm going to angle in
the camera or the one,

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:55.560
the butterflies folded up,

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:58.760
you can only see this brown
part, but when they open up,

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:01.010
they flash this really beautiful blue.

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:03.790
So they both have the
advantage of camouflage

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:06.880
and a flash of bright
color to potentially starve

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.593
their predators of food.

00:04:11.890 --> 00:04:15.330
So these were blue morphos,

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:16.833
I have another example of morphos

00:04:16.833 --> 00:04:18.733
just to show you the diversity.

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:23.100
These are actually different species

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.810
and these morphos are from
different parts of South America,

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:30.290
but it shows the variation that
you can see in the tropics.

00:04:30.290 --> 00:04:33.350
And so this is a different color pattern

00:04:33.350 --> 00:04:36.060
of the blue morpho but
a different species,

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.400
have a different wing shape.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:40.500
And this is an another
species it's very pearly

00:04:41.718 --> 00:04:43.820
and just like the other
species I showed you,

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:46.660
these morphos also have
a really interesting

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:50.903
underside pattern, which offers
them very good camouflage.

00:04:51.810 --> 00:04:54.560
So the next topic I'm going to mention,

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.210
that's very commonly
encountered in the tropics

00:04:57.210 --> 00:04:58.623
is something called mimicry.

00:04:59.871 --> 00:05:04.270
And mimicry is a topic
that is kind of complex,

00:05:04.270 --> 00:05:06.040
but it's really easy to understand

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:09.523
once you look at it more closely.

00:05:10.940 --> 00:05:12.330
So these butterflies here

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:14.500
are called heliconian butterflies

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:18.590
and heliconian butterflies
have caterpillars

00:05:18.590 --> 00:05:20.300
that feed on toxic plants.

00:05:20.300 --> 00:05:21.930
So when the caterpillars grow up,

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:24.800
when they pupate and
emerge as butterflies,

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:28.920
they will basically inherit
the caterpillar's toxicity

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:30.890
from eating those plants.

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:33.850
So when you have these
adults flying around,

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:36.390
these adults are gonna need to
be toxic to birds and mammals

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:38.730
and any other predators
that might want to eat them.

00:05:38.730 --> 00:05:41.580
So in some cases you actually have moths

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:44.160
and other insects that will actually mimic

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:48.160
the pattern of these
heliconian butterflies.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:51.300
So predators basically are
tricked into thinking that

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:54.040
they are distasteful
when they really aren't.

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:56.400
So you could as a something
that doesn't taste

00:05:56.400 --> 00:05:58.650
particularly bad, you could actually,

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:02.020
by using the coloration of
a heliconian trick predators

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:03.710
into not eating you.

00:06:03.710 --> 00:06:05.640
So this is actually a really common thing

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:07.280
amongst tropical butterflies.

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:09.680
And you actually have
these groups of species.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:11.870
It's a really good example
of evolution in action,

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:14.130
because you have even within a species,

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:15.300
all these different colors.

00:06:15.300 --> 00:06:17.340
So all of these are all the same species

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.420
of heliconian butterfly.

00:06:19.420 --> 00:06:23.080
And these heliconian butterflies
are basically adapted

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:25.140
to different areas of South America,

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:27.900
have different colored model species.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:29.590
So these model species
are different colors,

00:06:29.590 --> 00:06:32.460
so the mimics become
the same color as well.

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:33.900
And so over time you have

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:37.840
all these different associated
butterflies and moths

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:39.430
that kinda looked like each other

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:43.463
and also change as you go
from say, Colombia to Brazil.

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:47.733
So another example of
mimicry we see here is,

00:06:49.450 --> 00:06:51.130
these are all types of moths.

00:06:51.130 --> 00:06:54.880
So this is an assortment of
moths collected in the tropics.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:56.750
You can see moths are much more diverse

00:06:56.750 --> 00:07:00.700
in pattern, shape and
color than butterflies are.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:02.410
And they're also more species rich.

00:07:02.410 --> 00:07:05.820
There's many tens of thousands more moths

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:07.180
than there are butterflies,

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:09.990
pretty much anywhere you go
including the United States.

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:13.820
So this species in particular
is very interesting

00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:17.310
in that a lot of people
might think this is a wasp.

00:07:17.310 --> 00:07:19.190
This is actually a moth.

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:22.890
And this is a type of Batesian mimicry,

00:07:22.890 --> 00:07:27.060
which is basically a
harmless species of moth,

00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:30.220
mimics a species that's harmful a wasp.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:33.430
So through evolution, you have the,

00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:35.780
basically the adaptation of the moth

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:39.523
to evolve clear wings to look like a bee.

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:43.750
You can see here, it has clear wings,

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:45.300
you can see my hands through it

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:50.120
and has the really cool like yellow

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:53.230
and black striped pattern
characteristic of wasps.

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:56.240
So any predator that sees us in the wild

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:58.200
is going to think twice about eating it

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:01.810
because it's very much like a wasp.

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:04.710
A lot, even a lot of human
collectors are fooled by these

00:08:04.710 --> 00:08:08.420
as far as like what species is what.

00:08:08.420 --> 00:08:10.040
And you sometimes you have
to look really closely

00:08:10.040 --> 00:08:12.060
to be able to determine them.

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:17.060
My final box of moths here is,

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:22.000
these are examples of Urania,

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.930
which are actually day flying moths.

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:25.410
So you might think, what's the difference

00:08:25.410 --> 00:08:26.960
between a butterfly and a moth?

00:08:28.750 --> 00:08:31.630
A butterfly tends to fly during
the day and a moth at night.

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:35.180
Not necessarily, that's
probably true of 90% of them,

00:08:35.180 --> 00:08:37.640
but these are good examples of moths

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:40.060
that fly during the day
and they actually mimic

00:08:40.060 --> 00:08:44.860
swallowtail butterflies, which
have these nice projections

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:48.170
called tails, they call
them tails in a butterfly.

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:50.740
But these moths do a pretty done good job

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:53.690
of mimicking a butterfly.

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:55.080
And the only way you can really tell

00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:56.320
is by looking at their antenna.

00:08:56.320 --> 00:08:58.790
So on the antenna of this moth,

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:01.770
doesn't have a club tip a ball
on the tip of the antenna.

00:09:01.770 --> 00:09:04.190
Usually butterflies,
swallowtail butterflies

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:06.203
have that enlarged antenna tip,

00:09:07.120 --> 00:09:11.370
so they tend to be easy to tell
if you look at the antenna.

00:09:11.370 --> 00:09:14.300
So between lots of these
butterflies and moths,

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:17.820
you have a lot of
diversity in size as well.

00:09:17.820 --> 00:09:19.803
So this is a good example of a,

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:23.900
this is called a white witch
and this is a black which.

00:09:23.900 --> 00:09:25.650
You can see based on the scale,

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:28.320
this is the largest moth
in wing span in the world,

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.133
this white witch moth.

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:34.060
And the interesting thing about this

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:39.060
is that the caterpillar
was only recently like,

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:42.870
I think people had have photographed it,

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:45.850
but the caterpillar still
remains undescribed to this day.

00:09:45.850 --> 00:09:49.800
And if you've seen
caterpillars of large moths,

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:51.220
they could be pretty big.

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:53.430
So it remains to be seen that

00:09:53.430 --> 00:09:55.060
this caterpillar of this butterfly,

00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:57.480
which this isn't it, so unknown,

00:09:57.480 --> 00:09:59.520
if you could find the
caterpillar of this butterfly,

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:00.700
which is going to be or of this moth,

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:03.930
which is gonna be quite big,
you can contribute to science.

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:07.330
So even within our biggest
butterflies and moths species,

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:10.670
there's a lot of work that
could be done by even people,

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:12.680
even citizen scientists going out

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:14.090
and studying your local area.

00:10:14.090 --> 00:10:15.950
You could make contributions to science,

00:10:15.950 --> 00:10:17.953
even on stuff like this.

00:10:17.953 --> 00:10:20.536
(upbeat music)

